## 3. Why This Design?

Zig’s array/slice/pointer design solves several long‑standing C pain points while avoiding the complexity of C++ or Rust, all under the “no hidden control flow” umbrella.

### Arrays Don’t Decay

In C, an array name in an expression decays to a pointer to its first element, losing size information. Zig arrays are first‑class values. You can pass an array by value (copy), pass a pointer to it (`*[N]T`), or slice it (`[]T`). The programmer decides. There is no silent transition from array to pointer—if you want a slice, you write the slice operation.

### Slices Are the Universal Sequence View

A slice is a pointer‑plus‑length pair, exactly like Rust’s `&[T]`. It does not own memory, it has no capacity, and it cannot grow. This makes slices trivially safe to pass around without worrying about hidden allocations. If you need a growable sequence, use `std.ArrayList`, which is built around an allocator and exposes a slice view via `.items`. The separation of “view” (slice) and “owner” (ArrayList) is intentional: it forces explicit allocation points and prevents the “who frees this?” confusion.

### No Implicit Allocation for Concatenation

In many languages, `+` on slices/strings performs an allocation under the hood. Zig’s `++` operator works only on arrays and slices with comptime‑known lengths, producing a new array or a slice pointing to static memory. For runtime concatenation you call `std.mem.concat` or use an `ArrayList`; both require you to pass an allocator. This ensures that every allocation is visible in the source.

### Many-Item Pointers as an Escape Hatch

`[*]T` exists for two reasons: C ABI compatibility and low‑level pointer arithmetic. It is the only pointer type that allows arithmetic without bounds. Zig nudges you toward slices, which carry length and enable runtime safety. The existence of `[*]T` acknowledges that some code must interface with C or perform unsafe operations; the type name’s `[*]` syntax is a visible marker of “danger ahead.”

### Optional Pointers Instead of Implicit Null

C’s `NULL` is a valid pointer value that the type system does not distinguish. Zig’s `?*T` is a distinct type: you cannot dereference it without first checking for null. This eliminates null‑pointer dereference bugs from all but the most deliberate `@as` casts.

### Sentinels for Interop and Safety

The sentinel annotation (`[:0]T`, `[*:0]T`) allows Zig to interface with C’s null‑terminated strings without runtime overhead. The compiler can statically verify that array literals and string literals have the promised sentinel, and it can insert bounds‑check assertions in debug mode when constructing sentinel slices.

