## 6. Performance Considerations

### Array Copying Overhead

Passing a large array by value copies every element, incurring O(N) cost and potentially blowing the stack. Prefer passing a `*const [N]T` or a slice. The slice (16 bytes) is cheap to copy and still provides indexed access.

### Bounds Checking

In `Debug` and `ReleaseSafe` modes, slice indexing inserts a runtime bounds check. The cost is negligible for most code, but in the hottest loops you may see a few extra instructions. If you have profiled and verified that the check is a bottleneck, you can switch to `ReleaseFast` (which omits checks) or use `@setRuntimeSafety(false)` on a specific block. However, never do this prematurely—bounds checks have caught countless latent bugs.

An alternative for truly safety‑critical hot paths is to use a `[*]T` with manual length tracking and `@setRuntimeSafety` disabled, but this requires rigorous verification.

### Cache Locality

Slices provide a dense, contiguous memory view. Iterating over a slice with `for (slice) |item|` results in linear memory access, which the hardware prefetcher loves. This is the most cache‑friendly way to process sequences of data. The same holds for arrays, though a `for` over an array by value will copy each element unless you iterate by pointer: `for (&arr) |*item|`.

### Pointer vs. Slice as Function Parameters

Passing a slice costs 16 bytes (two registers). Passing a `*[N]T` costs 8 bytes (one register) but the length is fixed at compile time. If your function genuinely works with any length, the slice is the right choice; the extra register is free. If the length is a true constant (e.g., a cryptographic hash output size), `*const [32]u8` conveys that constraint to the type system and avoids runtime length loads.

### No Allocation Overhead for Slicing

Slicing is a pure register/stack operation; it never touches the heap. This makes it suitable for real‑time and embedded contexts where dynamic allocation is forbidden. You can slice stack arrays, static buffers, and memory‑mapped regions with equal ease.

